%[text] %[text:anchor:T_03bd] # Linear Systems Applications
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text] This live script is intended to be used with the code visible and output inline. On the **View** tab of the MATLAB Toolstrip, in the **View** section, select **Output Inline**. Alternately, select **Output Inline** using the icon ![live script output inline icon](text:image:4d0d) at the top right of the Live Editor pane.
%[text] ![Warning symbol](text:image:66c2)   For an optimal experience, follow the instructions and steps in the given sequence. Proceed to a new section only after completing the preceding one. Some sections depend on variables created in prior sections and they will generate errors if run out of order.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_21d2] ## Linear regression
%[text] Linear regression is a staple in traditional fields (physics and chemistry) and newer disciplines (machine learning and data science). This section will derive the least-squares solution to the linear regression problem.
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_H_62B7E440] **Data**
%[text] Suppose that you want to estimate a linear relationship for the random data shown below.
n = 31; %[control:slider:930f]{"position":[5,7]}
  %[control:button:503f]{"position":[1,2]}

x = sort(normrnd(5,1.75,n,1));
y = 1*normrnd(2,0.6,n,1) + x*(0.2+rand(1,1)/2);

scatter(x,y,"filled")
xlabel("x")
ylabel("y")
axis([0 10 0 10])
%[text] ![](text:image:2220) **Try.** Generate the data by running this section.
%%
%[text] ### **Residual**
%[text]  The relationship between $x${"altText":"x"} and $y${"altText":"y"} can be approximated by a linear function:
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $f(x) = w\_0 + w\_1 x${"altText":"f(x) = w\_0 + w\_1 x"}
%[text]  Typically, the best fit line is chosen to be the one that minimizes the sum of squares error. This error is called the residual.
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $R(w\_0,w\_1) = \\sum\_i \[ y\_i - (w\_0 + w\_1 x\_i) \]^2${"altText":"R(w\_0,w\_1) = sum over i of \[ y\_i - (w\_0 + w\_1 x\_i) \]^2"}
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_02A1A45E] Visually, the residual is computed by summing the squares of the vertical differences from the line.
w0 = 1.4; %[control:slider:31de]{"position":[6,9]}
w1 = 0.8; %[control:slider:4938]{"position":[6,9]}
  %[control:button:0f1d]{"position":[1,2]}

f = @(x) w0 + w1*x;
R = sum( (y - f(x)).^2 );

scatter(x,y,"filled")
hold on
plot([0 12],f([0 12]),SeriesIndex="none")
plot([x x]',[f(x) y]',SeriesIndex=2)
hold off

title("R(w_0,w_1) = " + num2str(R))
xlabel("x")
ylabel("y")
axis([0 10 0 10])
%[text] ![](text:image:5f8a) **Try.** Adjust the parameters of the linear function to reduce the sum of square error.
%%
%[text] ### **Writing the linear approximation in matrix form** 
%[text] To find the parameters $w\_1${"altText":"w\_1"} and $w\_2${"altText":"w\_2"} that minimize the residual $R${"altText":"R"}, you can solve for the least-squares solution.
%[text] To set up this system, write the data ($x\_i${"altText":"x\_i"} and $y\_i${"altText":"y\_i"}) in terms of vectors:
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\mathbf{x} = \\left( \\matrix{ x\_1 \\cr x\_2 \\cr \\vdots \\cr x\_n } \\right)${"altText":"x = ( matrix \[ x\_1 ; x\_2 ; ... ; x\_n \] )"}   and   $\\mathbf{y} = \\left( \\matrix{ y\_1 \\cr y\_2 \\cr \\vdots \\cr y\_n } \\right)${"altText":"y = ( matrix \[ y\_1 ; y\_2 ; ... ; y\_n \] )"}
%[text] Then write a linear approximation vector $\\tilde{\\mathbf{y}} = w\_0 + w\_1 \\mathbf{x}${"altText":"tilde(y) = w\_0 + w\_1 x"} as a linear combination:
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\tilde{\\mathbf{y}} = w\_0 \\left( \\matrix{ 1 \\cr 1 \\cr \\vdots \\cr 1} \\right)\n+ w\_1 \\left( \\matrix{ x\_1 \\cr x\_2 \\cr \\vdots \\cr x\_n } \\right)${"altText":"tilde(y) = w\_0 ( matrix \[ 1 ; 1 ; ... ; 1\] ) + w\_1 ( matrix \[ x\_1 ; x\_2 ; ... ; x\_n \] )"}    which is equivalent to the matrix form:     $\\tilde{\\mathbf{y}} = \\left( \\matrix{ 1 & x\_1 \\cr 1 & x\_2 \\cr \\vdots & \\vdots \\cr 1 & x\_n} \\right) \\left( \\matrix{ w\_0 \\cr w\_1 } \\right)\n${"altText":"tilde(y) = ( matrix \[ 1 , x\_1 ; 1 , x\_2 ; ... , ... ; 1 , x\_n\] ) ( matrix \[ w\_0 ; w\_1 \] )"}.
%[text] This can be written as
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\tilde\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{Xw}${"altText":"tilde(y) = Xw"}
%[text] where
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\mathbf{X} = \\left( \\matrix{ 1 & x\_1 \\cr 1 & x\_2 \\cr \\vdots & \\vdots \\cr 1 & x\_n} \\right) ${"altText":"X = ( matrix \[ 1 , x\_1 ; 1 , x\_2 ; ... , ... ; 1 , x\_n\] )"} and vector $\\mathbf{w} = \\left( \\matrix{ w\_0 \\cr w\_1 } \\right)${"altText":"w = ( matrix \[ w\_0 ; w\_1 \] )"}.
%[text] ![](text:image:0354) **Exercise**. In this exercise, you will write the approximate solution $\\tilde\\mathbf{y} ${"altText":"tilde(y)"} for the example [illustrated above](internal:M_02A1A45E) in matrix form using MATLAB.
%[text] 1\. Define the matrix $\\mathbf{X}${"altText":"X"} by concatenating the column of ones contained in the variable `u` with the vector `x` (which stores the data's $x${"altText":"x"}-locations). You can [concatenate](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/math/creating-and-concatenating-matrices.html#OverviewCreatingAndConcatenatingExample-3) two column vectors `a` and `b` using the syntax: `[a b]`.
u = ones(n,1); % A vector of ones
% Define X:

%[text] 2\. Define the vector $\\mathbf{w}${"altText":"w"} using the variables `w0` and `w1`.

%[text] 3\. Compute the linearly approximated y-values: $\\tilde\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{Xw}${"altText":"tilde(y) = Xw"}. Assign $\\tilde{\\mathbf{y}${"altText":"tilde(y)"} to the MATLAB variable `y_approx`.

%%
%[text] ### **Visualize the linear approximations of the data**
%[text] ![](text:image:6b8b) **Try.** Run this section to visualize the estimates stored in `y_approx`.
  %[control:button:87bf]{"position":[1,2]}
if exist("y_approx","var") 
    fls = @(x) w(1) + w(2)*x;
    R = sum( (y - fls(x)).^2 );

    scatter(x,y,"filled")
    hold on
    scatter(x,y_approx,'s',"filled")
    plot([0 12],fls([0 12]),":",SeriesIndex="none")
    plot([x x]',[fls(x) y]',":",SeriesIndex=2)
    hold off
    title("R(w_0,w_1) = " + num2str(R))
    xlabel("x")
    ylabel("y")
    leg = legend('Data',"$\tilde{\mathbf{y}}$");
    set(leg,"interpreter","latex")
    axis([0 10 0 10])
    
    chi=get(gca, 'Children');
    set(gca, 'Children',flipud(chi));
else
    warning("You must define y_approx before plotting it.")
end
%%
%[text] ### **Solution to the least-squares problem** 
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_H_620EDDDA] **General solution**
%[text] In the linear regression problem, you want to minimize the sum of square errors. The calculus approach is shown here. The residuals can be written in terms of the matrix elements as
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\begin{array}{rl}\nR(w\_0,w\_1) &= \\sum\_i \[ y\_i - (w\_0 + w\_1 x\_i) \]^2\n\\\\\n &= \\sum\_i (y\_i - \\tilde{y}\_i )^2\n\\\\\n &= \\sum\_i \\left(y\_i -  \\sum\_j X\_{ij} w\_{j} \\right)^2\n\\end{array}\n${"altText":"matrix \[ R(w\_0,w\_1)  = sum over i of \[ y\_i - (w\_0 + w\_1 x\_i) \]^2 ;  = sum over i of (y\_i - tilde(y)\_i )^2 ;  = sum over i of (y\_i - sum over j of X\_(ij) w\_(j) )^2 \]"}
%[text] To minimize this equation with respect to $w\_i${"altText":"w\_i"} using a calculus approach, find the stationary points. To do so, take the derivative $\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial w\_k}${"altText":"(partial)\/(partial w\_k)"} and set it equal to zero:
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\begin{array}{rl}\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial w\_k} R(w\_0,w\_1)  &= \\sum\_i 2\\left( y\_i - \\sum\_j X\_{ij} w\_{j} \\right) \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial w\_k} \\left(y\_i - \\sum\_j  X\_{ij} w\_{j} \\right)\n\\\\\n0 &= 2\\sum\_i  \\left( y\_i - \\sum\_j X\_{ij} w\_{j} \\right) ( - X\_{ik} )\n\\\\\n0 &= \\sum\_i  - y\_i X\_{ik} + \\sum\_{ij} X\_{ij} w\_{j} X\_{ik} \n\\end{array}\n${"altText":"system of equations: (partial)\/(partial w\_k) R(w\_0,w\_1)  = sum over i of 2( y\_i - sum over j of X\_(ij) w\_(j) ) (partial)\/(partial w\_k) (y\_i - sum over j of X\_(ij) w\_(j) ) ; 0  = 2sum over i of ( y\_i - sum over j of X\_(ij) w\_(j) ) ( - X\_(ik) ) ; 0  = sum over i of - y\_i X\_(ik) + sum over ij of X\_(ij) w\_(j) X\_(ik)"}
%[text] You can write the final equation in matrix notation as:
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\begin{array}{rl}\n0 &= -\\mathbf{y}^\\top \\mathbf{X} + \\mathbf{X}^\\top \\mathbf{X}\\mathbf{w} \n\\\\\n\\mathbf{X}^\\top \\mathbf{X}\\mathbf{w} & = \\mathbf{X}^\\top \\mathbf{y}\n\\end{array}${"altText":"system of equations: 0  = -y^T X + X^T Xw ; X^T Xw  = X^T y"}
%[text] The solution to this system, $\\mathbf{w}${"altText":"w"}, is a critical point of the residual function $R${"altText":"R"}. An additional argument (not shown here) is required to demonstrate that this minimizes the residual (this is intuitive due to the original quadratic form of the residual).
%[text] To solve this system for the coefficients $\\mathbf{w}${"altText":"w"}, you can use any standard method for solving a matrix system. Compare this result to the standard form of a linear system in matrix form:
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\mathbf{X}^\\top \\mathbf{X}\\mathbf{w}  = \\mathbf{X}^\\top \\mathbf{y}${"altText":"X^T Xw = X^T y"} $\\Leftrightarrow${"altText":"if and only if"}$\\mathbf{Aw} = \\mathbf{b}${"altText":"Aw = b"} 
%[text] These systems are equivalent with $\\mathbf{A} = \\mathbf{X}^\\top \\mathbf{X}${"altText":"A = X^T X"} and $\\mathbf{b} = \\mathbf{X}^\\top \\mathbf{y}${"altText":"b = X^T y"}.
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:0e49) **Exercise**. You can use the equation
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\mathbf{X}^\\top \\mathbf{X}\\mathbf{w}  = \\mathbf{X}^\\top \\mathbf{y}${"altText":"X^T Xw = X^T y"}
%[text] to find the least-squares solution of the linear regression problem. To do so, you can define the matrix $\\mathbf{A}${"altText":"A"} and right-hand side (RHS) vector $\\mathbf{b}${"altText":"b"} and then solve for the matrix solution. 
%[text] 1\. Compute the matrix `A` and RHS vector `b` using the formulas $\\mathbf{A} = \\mathbf{X}^\\top \\mathbf{X}${"altText":"A = X^T X"} and $\\mathbf{b} = \\mathbf{X}^\\top \\mathbf{y}${"altText":"b = X^T y"}.
%[text] **Hint**: Use an apostrophe (') to compute the [matrix transpose](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/double.ctranspose.html).


%[text] 2\. Compute matrix solution using the backslash operator. Save the result in `w`.

%[text] Note: you don't have to define `A` and `b` to compute the solution. You can use their expressions directly.
%%
%[text] ### **Visualize the least-squares fit**
%[text] ![](text:image:74b2) **Try.** Once your solution is complete (i.e., you have solved for $\\mathbf{w}${"altText":"w"}), you can view the least-squares solution by running this section.
  %[control:button:3cd7]{"position":[1,2]}
if exist("w","var")
    fls = @(x) w(1) + w(2)*x;
    R = sum( (y - fls(x)).^2 );
    scatter(x,y,"filled")
    hold on
    plot([0 12],fls([0 12]),SeriesIndex="none")
    plot([x x]',[fls(x) y]',SeriesIndex=2)
    hold off
    
    title("R(w_0,w_1) = " + num2str(R))
    xlabel("x")
    ylabel("y")
    axis([0 10 0 10])
else
    warning("Plot failed because w doesn't exist.")
end
%[text] - How close was your by-hand fit residual to the least-squares fit residual? \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_750d] ## Linear circuits
%[text] Simple linear circuits can be analyzed using Kirchhoff's circuit laws. Matrices provide a natural framework: they can concisely capture the many linear equations that model the circuit network. In this section, you will derive the matrix form of the linear equations that represent a linear circuit and solve for the currents in that circuit.
%[text] ### **Kirchhoff's current law**
%[text] The sum of all currents flowing into a node equals the sum of currents flowing out of the node.
%[text] 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Node in a circuit](text:image:7f3f)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *At this node, Kirchhoff's current law implies* $I\_1 = I\_2 + I\_3 + I\_4 ${"altText":"I\_1 = I\_2 + I\_3 + I\_4"}
%[text] ### **Kirchhoff's voltage law**
%[text] The sum of voltages around a closed-loop of a circuit is zero. Mathematically:
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\sum\_{k} V\_k = 0${"altText":"sum over k of V\_k = 0"}
%[text] where $V\_k${"altText":"V\_k"} is the potential difference across the $k^{th}${"altText":"k^(th)"} branch of the circuit. Applying Ohm's law gives the potential drop across each resistor: $V = IR${"altText":"V = IR"}.
%[text] 
%[text]{"align":"center"}  ![Closed loop in a circuit](text:image:025a)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *The potential drop across the resistors in this closed loop must equal the potential generated by the voltage source:* $V\_1 - I\_1 R\_1 - I\_1 R\_2 - I\_1 R\_3 = 0${"altText":"V\_1 - I\_1 R\_1 - I\_1 R\_2 - I\_1 R\_3 = 0"}
%%
%[text] #### **Matrix representation of a circuit**
%[text] In this section, the pictured linear circuit will be analyzed using Kirchhoff's laws, written in matrix form.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Circuit diagram](text:image:8f89)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *The circuit to be analyzed*
%[text] #### **Derivation of the equations**
%[text] Before writing the equations, it is useful to define current paths through the circuit. This provides a framework for applying Kirchhoff's laws. Note that the current paths are arbitrary, as current flowing in the opposite direction is represented by a negative value. The chosen reference directions are necessary to determine the correct form of Kirchhoff's laws.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Circuit diagram annotated to depict currents](text:image:06f5)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *In this diagram, three closed loops of the circuit are labeled (1), (2), and (3). The distinct currents in the circuit are also labeled.*
%[text] Applying **Kirchhoff's current law** to the central node yields:
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $I\_2 + I\_4 = I\_1 + I\_3${"altText":"I\_2 + I\_4 = I\_1 + I\_3"}
%[text] Applying **Kirchhoff's voltage law** to the three loops adds three additional equations:
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\begin{array}{rl} \n\\ V\_1 - I\_2 R\_1 - I\_2 R\_2  &= 0\n\\\\\n- V\_2 - I\_3 R\_3 - I\_4 R\_4  &= 0\n\\\\\n\\ V\_1 - I\_5 R\_5 - I\_4 R\_4  &= 0\n\\end{array}${"altText":"system of equations: V\_1 - I\_2 R\_1 - I\_2 R\_2  = 0 ; - V\_2 - I\_3 R\_3 - I\_4 R\_4  = 0 ; V\_1 - I\_5 R\_5 - I\_4 R\_4  = 0"}
%[text] At this point, you have 5 unknown currents but only four equations. To complete the representation, another application of Kirchhoff's current law is required. For the top node:
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $I\_1 = I\_2 + I\_5${"altText":"I\_1 = I\_2 + I\_5"}
%[text] Note that there are additional loops and nodes to which you can apply Kirchhoff's law – but all of them will yield redundant conditions.
%[text] #### **Derivation of the matrix form of the equations**
%[text] To write these equations in matrix form $\\mathbf{AI} = \\mathbf{b}${"altText":"AI = b"}, define the solution vector 
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\mathbf{I} = \\left( \\matrix{ I\_1 \\cr I\_2 \\cr I\_3 \\cr I\_4 \\cr I\_5 } \\right)${"altText":"I = ( matrix \[ I\_1 ; I\_2 ; I\_3 ; I\_4 ; I\_5 \] )"}
%[text] Moving all variable terms to the LHS and constant terms to the RHS implies:
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\begin{array}{rl} \n\\ I\_2 R\_1 + I\_2 R\_2  &= V\_1\n\\\\\nI\_3 R\_3 + I\_4 R\_4  &=  -V\_2\n\\\\\nI\_5 R\_5  + I\_4 R\_4  &= V\_1  \n\\\\\nI\_1 - I\_2 + I\_3 - I\_4 &= 0\n\\\\\nI\_1 - I\_2 - I\_5 &= 0\n\\end{array}${"altText":"system of equations: I\_2 R\_1 + I\_2 R\_2  = V\_1 ; I\_3 R\_3 + I\_4 R\_4  = -V\_2 ; I\_5 R\_5 + I\_4 R\_4  = V\_1 ; I\_1 - I\_2 + I\_3 - I\_4  = 0 ; I\_1 - I\_2 - I\_5  = 0"}        (1)
%[text] ![](text:image:749e) **Exercise**.
%[text] (a) Rewrite the system (1) in matrix form $\\mathbf{AI} = \\mathbf{b}${"altText":"AI = b"} by determining the form of $\\mathbf{A}${"altText":"A"} and $\\mathbf{b}${"altText":"b"}.
ShowSolution = false; %[control:checkbox:1cc8]{"position":[16,21]}
if(ShowSolution)
    ShowCircuitMatrices
end
%[text] (b) Compute the matrix product $\\mathbf{AI}${"altText":"AI"} to verify that the matrix form matches the original system (1).
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_H_1AC9C320] **Solutions to the matrix system**
%[text] **Specific solution.** 
%[text] ![](text:image:9d6b) **Exercise**. Suppose that the resistors and voltage sources have values:
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\begin{array}{l}\nR\_1 = 10 \\ \\Omega\n\\\\\nR\_2 = 100 \\ \\Omega\n\\\\\nR\_3 = 10 \\ \\Omega\n\\\\\nR\_4 = 1 \\ k\\Omega\n\\\\\nR\_5 =  330 \\ \\Omega\n\\end{array}${"altText":"system of equations: R\_1 = 10 Omega ; R\_2 = 100 Omega ; R\_3 = 10 Omega ; R\_4 = 1 kOmega ; R\_5 = 330 Omega"} $\\begin{array}{l}\nV\_1 = 1.5 \\ \\text{V}\n\\\\\nV\_2 = 1.5 \\ \\text{V}\n\\end{array}${"altText":"system of equations: V\_1 = 1.5 V ; V\_2 = 1.5 V"}
%[text] What are the currents through the circuit? Store the vector of currents in variable `I`. 
%[text] **Hints:** 
%[text] - Solve for the current by defining the numeric matrix `A`, the RHS vector `b`, and then applying the backslash operator ().
%[text] - Resistance is often given in terms of $\\Omega${"altText":"Omega"}, $k\\Omega${"altText":"kOmega"}, and $M\\Omega${"altText":"MOmega"}. \
% Write your code here

%[text]  This checks your solution against the expected answer
if exist("I","var")
    CurrentCheck1(I)
else
    warning("You must define I before you can check it.")
end
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:TMP_658c] **General solution.** 
%[text] ![](text:image:96b0) **Exercise**. Use symbolic math to find currents through this circuit for arbitrary resistances and voltages. 
%[text] Define symbolic variables for the resistances and voltages (e.g., `syms R_1 V_1` ).

%[text] (a) Solve for the current by defining a symbolic matrix `A` and RHS vector `b` in terms of the symbolic variables defined above. Then, apply the backslash operator () to solve for the currents. Store the currents in `Isym`.

%%
%[text] (b) Evaluate the general symbolic solution computed in (a) for the specific solution with parameters:
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\begin{array}{l}\nR\_1 = 10 \\ \\Omega\n\\\\\nR\_2 = 200 \\ \\Omega\n\\\\\nR\_3 = 10 \\ k \\Omega\n\\\\\nR\_4 = 2 \\ k\\Omega\n\\\\\nR\_5 =  330 \\ \\Omega\n\\end{array}${"altText":"system of equations: R\_1 = 10 Omega ; R\_2 = 200 Omega ; R\_3 = 10 k Omega ; R\_4 = 2 kOmega ; R\_5 = 330 Omega"} $\\begin{array}{l}\nV\_1 = 1.5 \\ \\text{V}\n\\\\\nV\_2 = 12 \\ \\text{V}\n\\end{array}${"altText":"system of equations: V\_1 = 1.5 V ; V\_2 = 12 V"}
%[text] Store the result in `Ispec`.
%[text] **Hint:** You can evaluate the symbolic solution for specific values of the variables in MATLAB. To do so, use [`matlabFunction`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/symbolic/sym.matlabfunction.html) to generate a MATLAB function handle and then evaluate it with the specific values of the parameters. This process is illustrated in an example below.
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_5dbc] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] syms x y                    % Symbolic variables
%[text] g = x^2 + y^2               % Symbolic function
%[text] gfunc = matlabFunction(g)   % MATLAB function handle
%[text] gfunc(2,3)                  % Evaluate the MATLAB function handle at (x,y)=(2,3)
%[text] ```
%[text] [This code](internal:M_5dbc) creates a symbolic function `g`, which is subsequently converted to a MATLAB function handle `gfunc`, which is then evaluated at $x = 2${"altText":"x = 2"} and $y = 3${"altText":"y = 3"}.
% Create a MATLAB function from the symbolic expression Isym

% Evaluate the function for the specified values, store the result in Ispec

%[text] This checks your solution against the expected answer
if exist("Ispec","var")
    CurrentCheck2(Ispec)
else
    warning("You must define Ispec before you can check it.")
end
